  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/b8a9560870e28c751b8869e298ffabdbcc23eec720c7a1057d55032a62767183.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=x+y+1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ but \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{y}{x+1}\\to \\frac{y}{2}<\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ and \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=|\\frac{y}{x-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
